<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>8: Gauss' law and the divergence theorem</title>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that we have an infinitely thin charged plate whose
  charge density is 13 Coulombs per square meter as shown on the
  left.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What does the symmetry of this situation imply about the
  direction of the electric field and electric displacement vectors?</p>
        <p>\begin{center}
    \includegraphics[scale=0.7]{figures/pillbox.png}
  \end{center}</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose we take a Gaussian surface that is a cylinder of radius <m>r</m>
  as shown on the right.  How much electric charge is contained within
  the cylinder?  (Remember that all the charge is on the plate.)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What does Gauss' Law tell us about the strength of the electric
  displacement <m>\mbf D</m> at a position <m>h</m> meters above the plate?
  (What can you say about the flux of <m>\mbf D</m> through the curvy sides
  of the cylinder?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that we want to create a potential <m>f</m> for the electric
  field <m>\mbf E</m> (remember that <m>\mbf D = \epsilon_0 \mbf E</m>.  Let's
  choose a basepoint <m>*</m> on the plate.  What is the value of the
  potential <m>h</m> meters above the plate?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that we have an infinitely thin, infinitely long charged
  wire whose charge density is <m>\lambda</m> Coulombs per meter.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Choose a
  cylindrical Gaussian surface of radius <m>r</m> and height <m>h</m> as shown
  below.</p>
        <p>\begin{center}
    \includegraphics[scale=0.7]{figures/wire.png}
  \end{center}</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How much electric charge is inside the surface?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What does the symmetry of the situation say about the direction of
  the electric field and electric displacement?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use Gauss' Law to find the electric displacement and electric field
  a distance <m>r</m> from the wire.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose we choose a basepoint <m>1</m> meter away from the wire.  Find
  the electric potential <m>r</m> meters away from the wire.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that we have a fluid whose velocity is given by the
  field <m>\mbf F(x,y,z)</m> meters per second.  Suppose that the density
  of the fluid is <m>\rho(x,y,z)</m> kilograms per meter<m>^3</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>E</m> is a three-dimensional region, what is the physical meaning
  of <m>\iiint_E \rho~dV</m>?  (Thinking about units will help.)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that <m>\mbf V = \rho \mbf F</m>.  What are the units on <m>V</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>S</m> is a surface, what is the physical meaning of <m>\iint_S \mbf
  V \cdot \mbf n~ dA</m> if <m>\mbf n</m> is a field normal to <m>S</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>S</m> is a closed surface with outward normal <m>\mbf n</m> bounding a
  region <m>E</m>, explain why 
  <md>
  \frac{d}{dt}\iiint_E \rho ~dV = -\oiint_S \mbf V\cdot\mbf n~dA,
  </md></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Explain why <m>\frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot \mbf
  V = 0</m>.   This is called the <em>equation of continuity</em> and is an
  expression of conservation of mass.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
